arXiv:2509.19702v1 [cs.LG] 24 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Linear Transformers Implicitly Discover
<br class="ltx_break">Unified Numerical Algorithms</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">
<span id="id1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="id1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Patrick Lutz<sup id="id1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup></span>
<span id="id1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Aditya Gangrade<sup id="id1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup></span></span>
<span id="id1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Boston University</span>
<span id="id1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Boston University</span></span>
<span id="id1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">plutz@bu.edu</span></span>
<span id="id1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gangrade@bu.edu</span></span></span>
<span id="id1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Hadi Daneshmand<sup id="id1.4.1.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup></span>
<span id="id1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Venkatesh Saligrama</span></span>
<span id="id1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">University of Virginia</span>
<span id="id1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Boston University</span></span>
<span id="id1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dhadi@virginia.edu</span></span>
<span id="id1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">srv@bu.edu</span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We train a <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">linear, attention-only</em> transformer on millions of <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">masked-block</span> completion tasks: each prompt is a masked low-rank matrix whose missing block may be (i) a scalar prediction target or (ii) an unseen kernel slice for Nyström extrapolation. The model sees only input–output pairs and a mean-squared loss; it is given no normal equations, no handcrafted iterations, and no hint that the tasks are related. Surprisingly, after training, algebraic unrolling reveals the <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same</em> parameter-free update rule across <em id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">three distinct computational regimes</em> (full visibility, rank-limited updates, and distributed computation). We prove that this rule achieves second-order convergence on full-batch problems, cuts distributed iteration complexity, and remains accurate with compute-limited attention. Thus, a transformer trained solely to patch missing blocks <em id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">implicitly discovers</em> a unified, resource-adaptive iterative solver spanning prediction, estimation, and Nyström extrapolation—highlighting a powerful capability of in-context learning.</p>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>These authors contributed equally to this work</span></span></span><span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Research was performed at BU under a NSF TRIPODS post-doctoral grant</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Models trained on next-token prediction achieve strong performance across various NLP tasks, including question answering, summarization, and translation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al.,, 2019</a>)</cite>. This multitask capability suggests an intriguing possibility: within structured mathematical contexts transformers might implicitly learn generalizable numerical algorithms solely through next-token prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next-token prediction can naturally be viewed as a form of matrix completion—inferring missing entries by exploiting dependencies in observed data. Prior work extensively explores matrix completion under computational constraints, such as distributed data access <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., (2020)</a></cite>, limited communication bandwidth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma and Chen, (2020)</a></cite>, and low-rank recovery from partial observations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Candès and Recht, (2009)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Candès and Tao, (2010)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Davenport and Romberg, (2016)</a></cite>. This raises a natural question: how do transformers implicitly handle such computational constraints to perform multitask learning?</p>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can a neural network <em id="S1.p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">invent</em> a numerical algorithm simply by learning to
fill in missing data?</span></p>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Transformers excel at <em id="S1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">in-context learning</em> (ICL), adapting to new tasks from a short prompt of examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., (2021)</a></cite>, and ICL serves as a simple subdomain to investigate the representational and learning properties of transformers. Recent studies suggest that for transformers trained on ICL tasks, the computations encoded by the weights resemble gradient-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al., (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al., (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., (2023)</a></cite>, typically in single-task scenarios. However, this analogy is limited: <em id="S1.p2.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gradient descent operates explicitly in parameter space, whereas transformers perform data-to-data transformations without direct access or updates to parameters</em>. Additionally, much of the exploratory focus in such work has been on the complexity of the regression task, while keeping the computational regime fixed. Thus, it remains unclear whether transformers implicitly develop distinct, unified numerical algorithms suited to diverse tasks and resource constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Masked-block Completion and Architectural Masks:</span>
To probe this question, we train a linear transformer on masked-block completion tasks that hide one block of a low-rank matrix mirroring the classical Nyström completion problem. The transformer’s task is to infer these missing blocks based solely on observable data and a mean-squared error objective—without explicit guidance about the underlying parameters or relationships between tasks. We impose three distinct architectural visibility constraints on transformers, each reflecting practical computational limitations common in large-scale optimization: <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">centralized</em> (full visibility), <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distributed</em> (restricted communication), and <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">computation-limited</em> (restricted complexity via low-dimensional attention). Each regime employs the same underlying transformer architecture, differing only minimally in their attention masks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emergence of a Unified Algorithm:</span> Remarkably, despite training independently under these distinct computational constraints, we find that transformers implicitly uncover the same concise, two-line iterative update rule. This unified algorithm, termed <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> emerges consistently across tasks and constraints, exhibiting strong theoretical and empirical properties: it achieves second-order convergence on full-batch problems, matches classical methods in centralized settings, significantly reduces communication complexity in distributed settings and remains accurate under computation-limited conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Summary of Contributions.</span> The main contributions of this work are</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unified masked-block completion benchmark:</span> A single training framework integrating multiple prediction or inference tasks into a unified interface.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Resource-aware transformer architectures:</span> Transformer-based models naturally adapt to centralized, distributed, and computation-limited environments through simple architectural constraints.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implicit numerical algorithm discovery:</span> Transformers implicitly discover a unified, efficient numerical solver, <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span>, that achieves second-order convergence on full-batch problems and consistently matches or outperforms classical methods across completion, extrapolation, and estimation tasks under varying resource constraints. Our theoretical results uniformly apply across all these tasks, highlighting the broad applicability of the discovered algorithm.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">These findings position transformers trained on block completion as powerful tools for uncovering numerical algorithms, offering a promising route toward developing adaptive, data-driven solvers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus on literature most pertinent to viewing transformers as fixed data-to-data transforms whose forward pass exposes emergent algorithms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implicit algorithm learning.</span>
Transformers have been shown to recover 1<sup id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sup">st</sup>- and 2<sup id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sup">nd</sup>-order methods for least squares, dual GD for optimal transport, and TD
updates for RL
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Daneshmand,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al.,, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al.,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giannou et al.,, 2023</a>)</cite>.
Weight-level circuit studies reverse-engineer copy-and-addition “induction
heads” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olah et al.,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nanda,, 2023</a>)</cite>, but are still
confined to token-manipulation algorithms.
RNNs and MLPs display related behavior
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegelmann and Sontag,, 1992</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong and Pehlevan,, 2024</a>)</cite>, yet attention yields
depth-aligned, easily inspected updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al.,, 2023</a>)</cite>.
Most prior work is therefore limited to first-order rules and a single,
centralized computational regime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task vs. regime.</span> Earlier in-context studies
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al.,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al.,, 2021</a>)</cite> vary the
regression task—sampling while the
hardware regime stays fixed. We take the opposite view: we <em id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fix</em> one
low-rank matrix-completion task and show that the same transformer discovers a
rule that adapts automatically as compute, memory, or communication
budgets are tightened.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Data-space vs. parameter-space.</span> Prior analyses tend interpret the forward pass of a transformer trained on an ICL task as updating an underlying hidden <em id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parameter</em> for some model of the training data relationships. However, in reality, a transformer is a <em id="S2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">data-to-data map</em>, with no explicit supervision of underlying model of parameters, which is the viewpoint we adopt. Weight-level ‘circuit’ studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olah et al.,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nanda,, 2023</a>)</cite> of trained transformers also take this view, but are focused on tasks like copying or addition; we instead are focused on numerical linear algebra tasks in varying computational settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-context regression limits.</span>
Theoretical lenses that view in-context learning as linear regression
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al.,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al.,, 2023</a>)</cite> recover
gradient-descent dynamics with <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> dependence on the condition number. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vladymyrov et al., (2024)</a></cite> show that, with suitable parametrization, preconditioning yields second-order dynamics and <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> convergence. We show that transformer training naturally converges to such parametrizations in practice, with this behavior robust to bandwidth and memory constraints. Theoretically, we prove that the recovered <span id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> method enjoys favorable guarantees hold across all studied settings, and further validate these empirically.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Links to classical numerical algorithms.</span> Structurally, the recovered <span id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> iterations bear intimate relationship to the Newton-Schulz method for inverting positive matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Higham,, 1997</a>)</cite>, but extend this to solve non-positive linear systems. In the centralised and sketched settings, this underlying relationship lends <span id="S2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> a second-order convergence rate, with iteration complexity depending only logarithmically on the condition number. This is particularly interesting since usual implementations of Nyström approximations for kernel methods all focus on Krylov-based solvers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams and Seeger,, 2001</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Halko et al.,, 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gittens and Mahoney,, 2016</a>)</cite>, and <span id="S2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> may lend new approaches to the same.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Distributed and sketched solvers.</span>
Block-CG, communication-avoiding Krylov
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Demmel et al.,, 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoefler et al.,, 2019</a>)</cite> and
sketch-and-solve techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clarkson and Woodruff,, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Woodruff,, 2014</a>)</cite>
dominate practice but remain first-order, and have net communication requirements scaling with the (joint) covariance of the data. We characterise the distributed <span id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> performance in terms of a ‘data diversity index’, <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,</annotation></semantics></math> and find significantly smaller communication (and iteration) complexity when <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is much smaller that this joint condition number.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explain how data are generated and encoded, how architectural constraints enforce three resource regimes, and how we extract an explicit numerical solver from trained weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Block Completion Setup.</span> We generate a low-rank matrix task by first drawing a matrix of the form</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\;=\;\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\\
B&amp;\hbox{\pagecolor{gray!25}$\displaystyle D$}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times(n+n^{\prime})},\quad\operatorname{rank}(X)=\operatorname{rank}(A)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFDFDF;" mathbackground="#DFDFDF">D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\;=\;\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\\
B&amp;\hbox{\pagecolor{gray!25}$\displaystyle D$}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times(n+n^{\prime})},\quad\operatorname{rank}(X)=\operatorname{rank}(A),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and then feeding the transformer a prompt matrix <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in which the lower-right grey block <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is masked, i.e., set to zero.
Given the visible blocks <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B,C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B,C</annotation></semantics></math>, the model must reconstruct
<math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with low <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> error. This single prompt template
generalizes Nyström extrapolation and scalar regression
(Appx. <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Methodological Details ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>). We train on both exact samples and noisy variants obtained by adding Gaussian noise with variance <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}\in\{0,10^{-2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}\in\{0,10^{-2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the low-rank assumption is approximate but realistic. Note that the Nyström approximation is the minimum-rank completion that matches observed entries, even if <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is full rank.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.19702v1/method.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/137;" width="476" height="137" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Architectural regimes studied in this work.<span id="S3.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
</span>(A)<span id="S3.F1.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <em id="S3.F1.5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Computation-limited</em>: a single-head attention-only transformer
whose query, key , value &amp; projections are restricted to a low-rank embedding of
dimension <math id="S3.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=r\ll n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=r\ll n</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., an explicit bottleneck that reduces the per-layer cost from
<math id="S3.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(n^{2}d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(n^{2}d)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(n\,r\,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(n\,r\,d)</annotation></semantics></math>; <span id="S3.F1.5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unconstrained.</span> The embedding dimension is set to <math id="S3.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=n+n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=n+n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span>(B)<span id="S3.F1.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <em id="S3.F1.5.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Distributed</em>: a multi-head transformer in which each head operates on data
stored on a separate machine; the heads run local attention and their outputs are
aggregated.
</span>(B<sup id="S3.F1.5.4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.F1.5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">′</span></sup>)<span id="S3.F1.5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <em id="S3.F1.5.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Per-machine routing</em>: detailed view of the distributed setting showing
how each machine <math id="S3.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\,\mu\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\,\mu\,</annotation></semantics></math> forms its local projection <math id="S3.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\mu}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\mu}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and contributes to the
next-layer representation <math id="S3.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data generation.</span>
We construct rank-<math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> data matrices <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times(n+n^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times(n+n^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math> by sampling <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+n^{\prime})\times s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+n^{\prime})\times s}</annotation></semantics></math>, each with rows drawn independently from <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, and setting <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=R_{1}R_{2}^{\top}/\sqrt{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>s</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=R_{1}R_{2}^{\top}/\sqrt{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To control the difficulty of the problem, we choose <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to be diagonal with entries <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{ii}=\alpha^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{ii}=\alpha^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, inducing strong anisotropy. Unless stated otherwise, we use the following parameters throughout: <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=d=18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=d=18</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}=d^{\prime}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}=d^{\prime}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.7</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields matrices <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with condition number on the order of <math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Half the time, we add Gaussian noise of variance <math id="S3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linear-attention transformer.</span>
We employ the linear-attention variant of transformers
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al.,, 2025</a>)</cite>.
Each layer <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> applies multi-head attention with a residual skip connection, to transform <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}=\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\\
B&amp;0\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}=\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\\
B&amp;0\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\ell}:=\begin{bmatrix}A_{\ell}&amp;C_{\ell}\\
B_{\ell}&amp;D_{\ell}\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\ell}:=\begin{bmatrix}A_{\ell}&amp;C_{\ell}\\
B_{\ell}&amp;D_{\ell}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> via the iterative structure</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{\ell+1}=Z_{\ell}+\sum_{h\in[1:H]}\textrm{Attn}_{\ell}^{h}(Z_{\ell}),\quad\textrm{Attn}_{\ell}^{h}(Z)=\bigl(ZW_{Q,\ell}^{h}(ZW_{K,\ell}^{h})^{\!\top}\odot M_{\ell}^{h}\bigr)Z\,W_{V,\ell}^{h}W_{P,\ell}^{h\,\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></munder><msubsup><mtext>Attn</mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mtext>Attn</mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>Z</mi><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.392em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\ell+1}=Z_{\ell}+\sum_{h\in[1:H]}\textrm{Attn}_{\ell}^{h}(Z_{\ell}),\quad\textrm{Attn}_{\ell}^{h}(Z)=\bigl(ZW_{Q,\ell}^{h}(ZW_{K,\ell}^{h})^{\!\top}\odot M_{\ell}^{h}\bigr)Z\,W_{V,\ell}^{h}W_{P,\ell}^{h\,\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q},W_{K},W_{V},W_{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q},W_{K},W_{V},W_{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> are query, key, value, and projection
matrices, and the fixed mask <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\ell}^{h}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{1}_{(d+d^{\prime})}\mathbf{1}_{d}^{\top}&amp;0_{(d+d^{\prime})\times d^{\prime}}\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\ell}^{h}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{1}_{(d+d^{\prime})}\mathbf{1}_{d}^{\top}&amp;0_{(d+d^{\prime})\times d^{\prime}}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> blocks the flow of information from the incomplete <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{\ell},D_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{\ell},D_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> column in <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> towards the visible column. Models are trained to minimize mean-squared error on <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>;
training details are in §<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Methodological Details ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
A schematic of the architecture appears in Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(A).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational Regimes via Architectural Constraints.</span> We study three distinct computational regimes— unconstrained (or centralized), computation-limited (low-dimensional attention), and distributed (restricted inter-machine communication)—each encoded into the architecture through explicit attention and dimensionality constraints. Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes these architectural regimes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unconstrained.</span>
No visibility or dimension constraints are imposed; each token attends to
all others. We set the embedding dimension <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=n+n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=n+n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Computation-Limited.</span> To emulate memory- or latency-bounded hardware we enforce a <em id="S3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">low-rank
attention</em> constraint: the query and key matrices have embedding size <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=r\ll n," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=r\ll n,</annotation></semantics></math> and the value and projection to <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (we use <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=5\approx n/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=5\approx n/4</annotation></semantics></math>; see
Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>A).
This bottleneck compresses the input and cuts the per-layer cost of recovered algorithms from
<math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(nd^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(nd^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(ndr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(ndr)</annotation></semantics></math>.
(See §<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Methodological Details ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for details).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Distributed Computation.</span>
The prompt <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> distributes <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> across <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> machines through a block structure</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{0}=\begin{bmatrix}\cdots&amp;X^{\mu}&amp;X^{\mu+1}&amp;\cdots\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times M(n+n^{\prime})},\textit{ where }X^{\mu}=\begin{bmatrix}A^{\mu}&amp;C\\[2.0pt]
B^{\mu}&amp;\hbox{\pagecolor{gray!25}$\displaystyle D$}\end{bmatrix},\mu\in[1:M]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> where </mtext><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFDFDF;" mathbackground="#DFDFDF">D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}=\begin{bmatrix}\cdots&amp;X^{\mu}&amp;X^{\mu+1}&amp;\cdots\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times M(n+n^{\prime})},\textit{ where }X^{\mu}=\begin{bmatrix}A^{\mu}&amp;C\\[2.0pt]
B^{\mu}&amp;\hbox{\pagecolor{gray!25}$\displaystyle D$}\end{bmatrix},\mu\in[1:M]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Each head is assigned to a machine <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, and all but the <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>th block in its query, key matrices are zeroed, enforcing local self-attention. Each <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> then ‘transmits’ information to others through the value, projection matrices, and incoming transmissions are summed to generate <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The full algebraic form, matrix partitioning and communication setups are provided in §<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Methodological Details ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm extraction.</span>
We generate explicit algorithms from a trained transformer by progressively simplifying its weights until a concrete update rule emerges. The steps taken are (also see §<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Methodological Details ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>):</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Weight quantization.</span> Cluster component values and sparsify matrices by dropping the values below <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. We then evaluate the performance with clustered weights to confirm no loss in performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Matrix Property Tests,</span> Check whether resulting matrices are random, sparse, or low-rank.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scaling Laws.</span> Identify how transformer scales attention and value blocks layer-by-layer.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In the unconstrained and compute-limited settings, an architecture with one head per layer suffices, while in the distributed setting, we use one head per machine.
After these simplifications every regime collapses to the <em id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same</em> two-line, parameter-free update rule (Eq. 2 in § 4), providing a direct algorithmic interpretation of the transformer’s in-context computation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Emergent Algorithm</h2>

<figure id="S4.T1.fig1" class="ltx_table ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_align_floatright" style="width:138.0pt;">
<table id="S4.T1.fig1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.fig1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">layer</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">U<math id="S4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">D<math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">C-L<math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.fig1.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.00</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.00</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.00</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.fig1.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2.36</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.62</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">0.01</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.fig1.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">5.27</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1.40</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">0.02</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.fig1.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">3</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">3.70</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1.28</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">0.13</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.fig1.3.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">4</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">2.16</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1.32</td>
<td id="S4.T1.fig1.3.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.14</td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.fig1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.fig1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Differences across iterations between transformer and extracted algorithm (squared Frobenius norm divided by size of <math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>) in the unconstrained (U), distributed (D), and compute-limited (C-L) settings. Mean <em id="S4.T1.fig1.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">times <math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></em> across 10 seeds is reported. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">One method, three resource regimes.</span>
Across all three architectural constraints, we find that the trained transformer solves the matrix completion task (Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) while implicitly implementing the <em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same</em> two-line scaling tranformation when its weights are algebraically interpreted.
Algorithm <a href="#algofigure1" title="List of algofigures 1 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> visualises the result, which we call <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> (Emergent Algorithm for Global Low-rank Estimation). The blue <span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Update</span> box is identical in all scenarios and across all machines, while the outer grey look captures the information fusion in the distributed setting (i.e., if <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>). The matrix <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> in <span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Update</span> is an orthogonal <em id="S4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sketching matrix</em> (see below). Table <a href="#S4.T1.fig1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that this extracted algorithm reproduces the exact layer-wise activations of the transformer to within <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\cdot 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\cdot 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> error, demonstrating its fidelity. We now discuss how this algorithm is derived in the three settings, focusing on the noiseless case. However, our findings remain valid under modest data noise (<math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, see §<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="algofigure1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algofigure ltx_framed ltx_framed_top"><img src="2509.19702v1/unify1.png" id="algofigure1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/154;" width="452" height="154" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algofigure1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">List of algofigures 1</span> </span><span id="algofigure1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Emergent Algorithm for Global Low-rank Estimation (<span id="algofigure1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span>).<span id="algofigure1.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<em id="algofigure1.5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Left:</em> the numerical kernel <span id="algofigure1.5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Update</span> runs unchanged on every
machine. <em id="algofigure1.5.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Right:</em> the outer loop calls <span id="algofigure1.5.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Update</span>, and in the distributed regime (<math id="algofigure1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M{&gt;}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M{&gt;}1</annotation></semantics></math>), it averages the resulting query updates (<math id="algofigure1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) and outputs <math id="algofigure1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="algofigure1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1</annotation></semantics></math> in the unconstrained and computation-limited settings. In the former, <math id="algofigure1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=I_{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=I_{n},</annotation></semantics></math> while in the latter, <math id="algofigure1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\smash{\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\smash{\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times r}}</annotation></semantics></math> is composed of random orthogonal rows. The values <math id="algofigure1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta,\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta,\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> are global constants (see below for their values).</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/legend.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/18;" width="238" height="18"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/performance.svg" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/344;" width="476" height="344"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Median test performance</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/cond.svg" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/351;" width="476" height="351"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Median condition number</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/parameters.svg" id="S4.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/351;" width="476" height="351"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Abstracted parameters</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The trained transformer solves matrix completion with a unified algorithm over all three computational settings. The evolution of key quantities throughout the transformer layers illustrate the remarkable similarity between the latent algorithms. Mean across 10 training seeds is reported. See §<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for plots with noisy data.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2509.19702v1/qk.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/110;" width="333" height="110" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2509.19702v1/value.png" id="S4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/110;" width="333" height="110" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Block structure of <math id="S4.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S4.F3.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">top</em>) and <math id="S4.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S4.F3.5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bottom</em>)
learned in the three regimes. Example shown for
<math id="S4.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,d,d^{\prime},n^{\prime})=(18,18,2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,d,d^{\prime},n^{\prime})=(18,18,2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>; distributed run uses <math id="S4.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=3</annotation></semantics></math> workers with
per-worker <math id="S4.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, and all of the <math id="S4.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{W_{VP}^{\mu}\}_{\mu=1,2,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.250em" minsize="1.250em">{</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.250em" minsize="1.250em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{W_{VP}^{\mu}\}_{\mu=1,2,3}</annotation></semantics></math> are collated together with each of the three block-wise rows corresponding to one head, while omitting all (null) non-<math id="S4.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> blocks in <math id="S4.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>. Off-diagonal blocks appear <em id="S4.F3.5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only</em> in <math id="S4.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>s, and are identical suggesting structure of messaging. Statistics over 10 seeds are in §<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unconstrained setting.</span>
Because token updates are not restricted, the weight patterns are the
cleanest to interpret here, and will re-appear in the
distributed and compute-limited regimes.</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Emergent weight structure.</span>
During the extraction procedure we progressively pruned attention heads and
quantised weights while tracking validation loss.
Remarkably, <em id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">one head per layer</em> is already sufficient: pruning from
eight to a single head has little effect on test MSE. After pruning, the weight products
<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{W_{Q,\ell}W_{K,\ell}^{\!\top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{W_{Q,\ell}W_{K,\ell}^{\!\top}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{W_{V,\ell}W_{P,\ell}^{\!\top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{W_{V,\ell}W_{P,\ell}^{\!\top}}</annotation></semantics></math> collapse to (almost) diagonal matrices.</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}:=W_{Q,\ell}W_{K,\ell}^{\!\top}\;\approx\;\operatorname{diag}\!\bigl(\alpha_{\ell}^{1}I_{n},\;0_{n^{\prime}}\bigr),\qquad W_{VP,\ell}:=W_{V,\ell}W_{P,\ell}^{\!\top}\;\approx\;\operatorname{diag}\!\bigl(\alpha_{\ell}^{2}I_{n},\;\alpha_{\ell}^{3}I_{n^{\prime}}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.736em;" width="1.736em"><mi>diag</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mn> 0</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.736em;" width="1.736em"><mi>diag</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}:=W_{Q,\ell}W_{K,\ell}^{\!\top}\;\approx\;\operatorname{diag}\!\bigl(\alpha_{\ell}^{1}I_{n},\;0_{n^{\prime}}\bigr),\qquad W_{VP,\ell}:=W_{V,\ell}W_{P,\ell}^{\!\top}\;\approx\;\operatorname{diag}\!\bigl(\alpha_{\ell}^{2}I_{n},\;\alpha_{\ell}^{3}I_{n^{\prime}}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where only the three scalars
<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{\alpha_{\ell}^{1,2,3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{\alpha_{\ell}^{1,2,3}}</annotation></semantics></math> vary from layer to layer.
Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (left) visualises a typical pair; layer-wise
statistics across 10 seeds are reported in §<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
This near-block-diagonal structure lets us algebraically reduce the transformer’s forward step to the update shown in Fig. <a href="#algofigure1" title="List of algofigures 1 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">From weights to <span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Update</span>.</span>
The additive steps in <span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Update</span> are due to the residual connection. Further, the diagonal forms of <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> imply that (see §<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>), the
attention block acts as</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Attn}_{\ell}(Z_{\ell})=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{2}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\!\top}A_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{3}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\!\top}C_{\ell}\\[3.0pt]
\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{2}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\!\top}A_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{3}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\!\top}C_{\ell}\end{bmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Attn</mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Attn}_{\ell}(Z_{\ell})=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{2}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\!\top}A_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{3}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\!\top}C_{\ell}\\[3.0pt]
\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{2}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\!\top}A_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{3}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\!\top}C_{\ell}\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The form of the iterative update in Alg. <a href="#algofigure1" title="List of algofigures 1 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1,S=I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1,S=I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is then immediate.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Rationale for Weights</em>. Further, the values <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\ell}^{1,2,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\ell}^{1,2,3}</annotation></semantics></math> see two persistent structural effects: throughout, <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{2}\approx\eta\|A_{\ell}\|_{2}^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{2}\approx\eta\|A_{\ell}\|_{2}^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{3}\approx\gamma\|A_{\ell}\|_{2}^{-2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{3}\approx\gamma\|A_{\ell}\|_{2}^{-2},</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A_{\ell}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A_{\ell}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectral norm of <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., largest singular value, tuned to the largest such value typically seen in a training batch), and <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma,\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma,\eta</annotation></semantics></math> are the <em id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constants</em> <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\approx 1.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\approx 1.9</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the scale of these weights is determined by <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A_{\ell}\|^{-2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A_{\ell}\|^{-2},</annotation></semantics></math> which is used in a fixed way by the method. Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{2}\|A_{\ell}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\ell}^{1}\alpha_{\ell}^{2}\|A_{\ell}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is constant across layers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Understanding the method</em>. Note that the transformation <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\mapsto(I-\rho\eta AA^{\top})A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\mapsto(I-\rho\eta AA^{\top})A</annotation></semantics></math> contracts large singular values of <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> more than small singular values. The net effect of this repeated action on <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is that for large <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, all initially nonzero singular values of <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> converge to one another, i.e., the matrix <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> becomes well-conditioned (see Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). This aspect of the method is reminiscent of the Newton-Schulz method for matrix inversion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulz,, 1933</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ben-Israel,, 1965</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Higham,, 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>. The overall structure of the iterations can then be seen as a ‘continuous conditioning update’ for <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. The iterations for <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\ell},D_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\ell},D_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are reminiscent of gradient descent, adapted to the varying <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Relation to prior work.</span>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al., (2023)</a></cite> dubbed the same update “GD<sup id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">++</span></sup>” in the
scalar case <math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d^{\prime}\!=\!n^{\prime}\!=\!1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d^{\prime}\!=\!n^{\prime}\!=\!1)</annotation></semantics></math> and interpreted it as pre-conditioned
gradient descent. In our opinion, there are two deficiencies in this viewing of the method. Firstly, gradient based methods implicitly assume that one is optimising a parameter (i.e., searching for a <math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\approx WA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\approx WA</annotation></semantics></math>, and imputing <math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\approx WC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\approx WC</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas the transformer has not been supervised in a manner that reveals the existence of an underlying parameter. In other words, a gradient descent type interpretation is not a behavioural (in the sense of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Willems, (1991)</a></cite>) description of the recovered method. Secondly, our analysis shows that unlike gradient descent, the recovered method is closer to a
<em id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Newton–Schulz conditioning loop</em> wrapped around direct prediction. For these reasons, rather than calling this underlying method “GD<sup id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">++</span></sup>”, we
refer to it as the <span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> method in the rest of the
paper. §<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> contrasts the two viewpoints in detail.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distributed setting.</span>
Following the unconstrained setting, we train with a single head per machine.</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Communication Structure</em> As detailed in §<a href="#S3" title="3 Method ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, for each head, the block structure of the <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((n+n^{\prime}))\times(M(n+n^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((n+n^{\prime}))\times(M(n+n^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> value-projection matrix <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> governs what machine <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sends</em> to other machines. Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (middle) reveals two striking regularities in this matrix.</p>
<ul id="S4.I2.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Within-machine blocks.</span>
Every diagonal block equals <math id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{diag}(\alpha_{\ell}^{1}I_{n},\;\alpha_{\ell}^{2}I_{n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{diag}(\alpha_{\ell}^{1}I_{n},\;\alpha_{\ell}^{2}I_{n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>—exactly
the same form as in the unconstrained model, with the <em id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same</em>
pair <math id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha^{1}_{\ell},\alpha^{2}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha^{1}_{\ell},\alpha^{2}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> across all machines.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Across-machine blocks.</span>
Off-diagonal blocks are
<math id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{diag}(0_{n},\;\alpha^{2}_{\ell}I_{n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{diag}(0_{n},\;\alpha^{2}_{\ell}I_{n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>: i.e. only the
incomplete columns <math id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{\ell},D_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{\ell},D_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> are transmitted, and with the
very same factor <math id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{2}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{2}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> as the within machine blocks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Resulting algorithm.</span> Since <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and the local block of <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same structure as the unconstrained setting, each machine executes the same local iteration as in the <span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Update</span> method. Further, via the off-diagonal blocks in <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}^{\mu}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}^{\mu},</annotation></semantics></math> each machine transmits only the <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{\prime}(d+d^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{\prime}(d+d^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> entries of its update to <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\ell},D_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\ell},D_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, captured in the <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime},D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime},D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> outputs in Alg. <a href="#algofigure1" title="List of algofigures 1 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Since attention heads are simply added, every machine averages its received blocks, leading to
<math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{C_{\ell+1}=M^{-1}\!\sum_{\mu}C^{\prime\mu}},\;\smash{D_{\ell+1}=M^{-1}\!\sum_{\mu}D^{\prime\mu}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>μ</mi></msub><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>μ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>μ</mi></msub><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>μ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{C_{\ell+1}=M^{-1}\!\sum_{\mu}C^{\prime\mu}},\;\smash{D_{\ell+1}=M^{-1}\!\sum_{\mu}D^{\prime\mu}}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The shared columns are thus identical across all <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> at all <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, recovering Alg. <a href="#algofigure1" title="List of algofigures 1 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Communication cost.</span>
Under the star topology in Alg. <a href="#algofigure1" title="List of algofigures 1 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> each machine communicates <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\bigl((d+d^{\prime})\,n^{\prime}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\bigl((d+d^{\prime})\,n^{\prime}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> floats per round; for scalar prediction
<math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d^{\prime}=n^{\prime}=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d^{\prime}=n^{\prime}=1)</annotation></semantics></math> this is the advertised <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math>-float message.
Notably, the transformer recovers this sparse pattern <em id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without</em> any explicit communication constraint. Exploring how stronger topological or bandwidth limits shape the learned algorithm is an open problem.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computation-limited setting.</span>
Here the query, key, value and projection matrices are rank-constrained: <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q,\ell},W_{K,\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q,\ell},W_{K,\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V,\ell},W_{P,\ell}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times(r+n^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V,\ell},W_{P,\ell}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times(r+n^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll n</annotation></semantics></math>. Similar low-rank constraints commonly arise when attention head dimensions are smaller than the transformer’s overall embedding size.</p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Emergent weight structure.</span>
Again, only one head per layer is needed. The <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+n^{\prime})\times(n+n^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+n^{\prime})\times(n+n^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> matrices <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> share three universal properties (Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)</p>
<ul id="S4.I3.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Block-diagonal form.</span>
Both are block-diagonal; the cross blocks
<math id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n\times n^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n\times n^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n^{\prime}\times n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n^{\prime}\times n)</annotation></semantics></math> vanish as in the unconstrained
regime. The <math id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n^{\prime}\times n^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n^{\prime}\times n^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> block of <math id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
scaled identity, exactly as before.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rank-<math id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> top left block.</span>
The leading <math id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> block of each matrix has numerical rank
<math id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(&lt;n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mspace style="width:0.389em;" width="0.389em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(&lt;n)</annotation></semantics></math> and the two blocks are similar up to a sign when rescaled to spectral norm 1 (relative difference: 0.28).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Random sketch spectrum.</span>
The eigenvalues and entry distribution of the leading block match
those of <math id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SS^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SS^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a random <math id="S4.I3.i1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times r</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal matrix
(Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sketching interpretation.</span>
The transformer therefore materializes an <em id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">orthogonal row sketch</em> <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> within the ‘top left’ blocks of its <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. This sketch acts upon the columns within the <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{\ell},B_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{\ell},B_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> blocks of the state <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the output of the attention block is structured as</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Attn}_{\ell}(Z)=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha^{1}(AS)(AS)^{\!\top}(AS)S^{\top}&amp;\ \alpha^{2}(AS)(AS)^{\top}C\\
\alpha^{1}(BS)(AS)^{\!\top}(AS)S^{\top}&amp;\alpha^{2}(BS)(AS)^{\top}C\end{bmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Attn</mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Attn}_{\ell}(Z)=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha^{1}(AS)(AS)^{\!\top}(AS)S^{\top}&amp;\ \alpha^{2}(AS)(AS)^{\top}C\\
\alpha^{1}(BS)(AS)^{\!\top}(AS)S^{\top}&amp;\alpha^{2}(BS)(AS)^{\top}C\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{1},\alpha^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{1},\alpha^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are constants depending on <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, the update first computes the sketches <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AS,BS\in\smash{\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AS,BS\in\smash{\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the ‘complete’ columns, and then proceeds with the update as in the unconstrained case, lifting them back to <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times d</annotation></semantics></math> by the terminal <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Setting <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#algofigure1" title="List of algofigures 1 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> recovers the exact layer dynamics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What the sketch buys.</span>
The contraction still needs
<math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\ell}=\|\tilde{A}_{\ell}\|_{2}^{-2}/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\ell}=\|\tilde{A}_{\ell}\|_{2}^{-2}/3</annotation></semantics></math> but now
<math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{A}_{\ell}=AS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{A}_{\ell}=AS</annotation></semantics></math> is only <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> columns wide, reducing the per-layer flop count from <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{2}d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{2}d)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(nrd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(nrd)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although this sketched update causes the iteration complexity of the method to increase, the overall hope is that the lower per-iteration cost may translate into a better total runtime. This aligns with the original motivation in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., (2017)</a></cite>, where low-rank constraints on <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{K,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{K,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{P,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{P,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> were explicitly introduced for computational efficiency.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F5.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:200.1pt;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/legend_histplot.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:169/16;" width="169" height="16"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/sv.svg" id="S4.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:276/208;" width="276" height="208"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/offdiag.svg" id="S4.F5.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:276/203;" width="276" height="203"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.fig3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.fig3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The computation-limited transformer implements pseudo-random sketching. The figure matches the candidate sketch matrices across all layers against common sketch characteristics (randomness, clustered eigenvalues). Left: Distribution of eigenvalues. Right: Distribution of coefficients.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F5.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:138.0pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/norm_save.svg" id="S4.F5.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:134/105;" width="134" height="105"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.fig4.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.fig4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The transformer learns to normalize the batch-maximum spectral norm of the iterate <math id="S4.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> observed during training. The plot reports <math id="S4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\ell}\beta_{\ell}\max_{b\in[B]}\|A_{\ell}^{(b)}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\ell}\beta_{\ell}\max_{b\in[B]}\|A_{\ell}^{(b)}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the maximum is taken over each batch. Results are averaged over 10 training seeds.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Evaluation of <span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having identified the <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> update underlying the transformer weights, we move on to studying how well the extracted algorithm performs in the three regimes consider, both theoretically and empirically. All ablations are deferred to §<a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Further Details on Evaluation of EAGLE ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, while theoretical proofs appear in §<a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Theoretical Analyses ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>. Unless stated otherwise, all numerical evaluations in this section use data sizes <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=d=240" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=d=240</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}=d^{\prime}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}=d^{\prime}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, condition number <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(A)=10^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(A)=10^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rank}(A)=240" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rank}(A)=240</annotation></semantics></math> and average performance across 50 runs is reported.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Unconstrained (centralized) setting</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second-order convergence guarantee.</span> We begin by discussing the theoretical convergence properties of <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> in the centralised case. Let <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(M)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the condition number of a matrix <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. The main result is</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Nyström estimate for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D.</annotation></semantics></math> If <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1/3,\gamma=1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1/3,\gamma=1,</annotation></semantics></math> then under <span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=I_{n},M=1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=I_{n},M=1,</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math> there exists</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=O\!\bigl(\log\kappa+\log\log(\varepsilon^{-1}\sqrt{d^{\prime}}\|W_{*}\|_{F}\|C\|_{F})\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi>O</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=O\!\bigl(\log\kappa+\log\log(\varepsilon^{-1}\sqrt{d^{\prime}}\|W_{*}\|_{F}\|C\|_{F})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\ell\geq L,\|D_{L}-\hat{D}_{\star}\|_{F}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⋆</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\ell\geq L,\|D_{L}-\hat{D}_{\star}\|_{F}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{*}=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{*}=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Nyström parameter (§<a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Nyström Approximation ‣ Appendix A Methodological Details ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mechanism.</em> We show Thm. <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 5.1 Unconstrained (centralized) setting ‣ 5 Evaluation of EAGLE ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in §<a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Theoretical Analyses ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> by arguing that each iteration shrinks <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\ell}:=\kappa(A_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\ell}:=\kappa(A_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> by at least a constant factor, and that once <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\ell}\leq 2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\ell}\leq 2,</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\ell}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\ell}-1</annotation></semantics></math> decyas supergeometrically. Error is controlled by developing a telescoping series with terms decaying with <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\kappa_{\ell}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\kappa_{\ell}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\log(\varepsilon^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\log(\varepsilon^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> dependence in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the quadratic rate, is related to the quadratic decay in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\ell}-1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\ell}-1,</annotation></semantics></math> which in turn arises since the <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> iterations for <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> bear a strong relationship to the classical Newton-Schulz method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Higham,, 2008</a>, Ch. 5,7)</cite>.
We note that a simple <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|D_{\ell}-D_{\ell-1}\|_{F}\!&lt;\!\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|D_{\ell}-D_{\ell-1}\|_{F}\!&lt;\!\tau</annotation></semantics></math> stopping rule suffices in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Positioning vs. classics.</span>
Direct inversion via QR-decomposition or SVD costs <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{O(\min n^{2}d,d^{2}n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{O(\min n^{2}d,d^{2}n)}</annotation></semantics></math> once, independent of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.
Krylov methods such as the Conjugate Gradient method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hestenes and Stiefel,, 1952</a>, CG,)</cite> and gradient descent (GD) need
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\log(\varepsilon^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\log(\varepsilon^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa^{2}\log(\varepsilon^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa^{2}\log(\varepsilon^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> iterations and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(nd)</annotation></semantics></math>
time per-iteration to compute matrix–vector products.
By contrast, <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> achieves quadratic
convergence with iteration counts scaling only with <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>, albeit with <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min(n^{2}d,d^{2}n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min(n^{2}d,d^{2}n)</annotation></semantics></math> cost per iteration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical protocol.</span> To evaluate the empirical performance of the recovered algorithm, we benchmark <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> against a QR-based solver (<span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.linalg.lstsq</span>), the Conjugate Gradient method and gradient descent (GD) on synthetic
<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Appx. <a href="#A3.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ Appendix C Parameter Estimation ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> for details). In particular, we study</p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Error vs. iteration (log–log).</span>
<math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\!=\!n\!=\!240" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>240</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\!=\!n\!=\!240</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\!=\!10^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\!=\!10^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>;
confirms slope <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 2</annotation></semantics></math> predicted by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 5.1 Unconstrained (centralized) setting ‣ 5 Evaluation of EAGLE ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-sweep.</span>
Time to reach <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\!=\!10^{-20}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\!=\!10^{-20}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\!\in\!\{10^{2},\dots,10^{5}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\!\in\!\{10^{2},\dots,10^{5}\}</annotation></semantics></math>;
visualises the <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> gap.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rank-deficient check.</span>
Time to reach <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=10^{-20}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=10^{-20}</annotation></semantics></math> highlights robustness to rank-deficiency.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 6 Conclusions ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> present (1)–(3);
rank-deficient and extended size sweeps are in §<a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Further Details on Evaluation of EAGLE ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Take-away.</span>
The transformer-extracted update converges <em id="S5.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quadratically</em> with
only a <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> penalty— <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim 100\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 100\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer
iterations than CG at <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\!=\!10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\!=\!10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>—while retaining
<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\min(nd^{3},dn^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\min(nd^{3},dn^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math>-per-iteration cost.
This positions <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> in a previously unexplored region of the speed–accuracy trade-off: an iterative solver that matches QR-based performance up to a <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>log</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log</annotation></semantics></math> factor.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Distributed setting</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diversity drives the rate.</span>
Besides the local condition numbers
<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa^{\mu}\!=\!\kappa(A^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa^{\mu}\!=\!\kappa(A^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math>, convergence depends on how
<em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distinct</em> the column spaces on different workers are. We capture this via:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(<span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diversity index</span>) Normalise <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A^{\mu}\|_{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A^{\mu}\|_{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a projection onto the column space of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>. The diversity index of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A^{\mu}\}_{\mu=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A^{\mu}\}_{\mu=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha\;:=\;\min_{\|v\|=1}M^{-1}\sum_{\mu\in[1:M]}\|P^{\mu}v\|_{2}^{\,2}\quad\in(0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mi>v</mi><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn> 2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.0835em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\;:=\;\min_{\|v\|=1}M^{-1}\sum_{\mu\in[1:M]}\|P^{\mu}v\|_{2}^{\,2}\quad\in(0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A large overlap in subspaces captured by the distinct machines <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> causes a reduction in <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,</annotation></semantics></math> and orthogonal subspaces induce <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>. This diversity index <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> captures the convergence scale of distributed <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> via the following result shown in §<a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Theoretical Analyses ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\max}=\max_{\mu}\kappa^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\max}=\max_{\mu}\kappa^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the noiseless case low rank case, i.e., when <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists W_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists W_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}B&amp;D\end{bmatrix}=W_{*}\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}B&amp;D\end{bmatrix}=W_{*}\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> with <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1/3,\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1/3,\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that for all <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=O(\log(\kappa_{\max}+\alpha^{-1}\log(\sqrt{d^{\prime}}\|C\|_{F}\|W_{*}\|_{F}/\varepsilon)\textit{ such that }\ell\geq L\implies\|D_{L}-D\|_{F}\leq\varepsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> such that </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=O(\log(\kappa_{\max}+\alpha^{-1}\log(\sqrt{d^{\prime}}\|C\|_{F}\|W_{*}\|_{F}/\varepsilon)\textit{ such that }\ell\geq L\implies\|D_{L}-D\|_{F}\leq\varepsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mechanism.</em> After <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\kappa_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\kappa_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, every local <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> has
near-unit singular spectrum. Subsequent progress is controlled by the condition number of the <em id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">average energy matrix</em> <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{\bar{E}_{\ell}=M^{-1}\sum_{\mu}A_{\ell}^{\mu}A_{\ell}^{\mu\top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>μ</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{\bar{E}_{\ell}=M^{-1}\sum_{\mu}A_{\ell}^{\mu}A_{\ell}^{\mu\top}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which tends to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Second-order convergence re-emerges when <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us note that <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is always smaller than <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(\bar{E}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(\bar{E}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and, depending on how the data is distributed across machines, may be much smaller than the same.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> Our main interest is in comparing with distributed baselines that operate with <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d)</annotation></semantics></math> units of communication per round—any more, and we may simply share the data across all machines into one central server. For this reason, QR decompositions and the Conjugate Gradient method are unavailable, which leaves gradient descent (GD) as the main competitor. Both GD and <span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> have the identical communication costs, transmitting <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O((d+d^{\prime})n^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O((d+d^{\prime})n^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> floats per iteration. However, the iteration complexity of GD scales with <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(\bar{E}_{0})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(\bar{E}_{0}),</annotation></semantics></math> which is always <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;\alpha^{-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;\alpha^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math> and may be much larger. As a result, <span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> has a strong advantage in practical strongly communication-limited scenarios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:36%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/distr_m_withdiv.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:172/122;" width="172" height="122"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S5.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Iteration complexity of distributed <span id="S5.F6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> is
independent of the number of workers <math id="S5.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical protocol.</span>
We vary the two rate-determining parameters, <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M,\alpha," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M,\alpha,</annotation></semantics></math> and report error against iteration in Figs. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.2 Distributed setting ‣ 5 Evaluation of EAGLE ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>,<a href="#S6.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 6 Conclusions ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a></p>
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>-sweep.</em> <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\!=\!1,3,5,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\!=\!1,3,5,8</annotation></semantics></math> workers on fixed total data
(<math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=n=1000,\;\kappa_{\max}=10^{3},\;\alpha\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=n=1000,\;\kappa_{\max}=10^{3},\;\alpha\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-sweep.</span> Four synthetic data sets with fixed <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{1,0.9,0.36,0.004\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.36</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.004</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{1,0.9,0.36,0.004\}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Take-away.</span>
<span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span>  reaches <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math> iterations for every
<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\!\in\!\{1,3,5,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\!\in\!\{1,3,5,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> when the worker subspaces are nearly orthogonal
(<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math>), and its iteration count grows exactly linearly with
<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> as overlap increases (Fig. <a href="#S6.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 6 Conclusions ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Further, for controlled <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the resulting iteration complexity is independent of the number of workers <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.2 Distributed setting ‣ 5 Evaluation of EAGLE ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). This confirms our theoretical result. In contrast first-order GD require 10–100<math id="S5.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> more rounds.
Noise experiments and a “best-of-both” hybrid
(<span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> until <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> stabilizes, then GD) are detailed in §<a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Further Details on Evaluation of EAGLE ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Computation-limited sketching</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn to iterations driven by the sketched columns
<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{A}_{\ell}:=S_{\ell}^{\top}A,\;\tilde{B}_{\ell}:=S_{\ell}^{\top}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{A}_{\ell}:=S_{\ell}^{\top}A,\;\tilde{B}_{\ell}:=S_{\ell}^{\top}B</annotation></semantics></math> via the i.i.d. orthogonal sketches
<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\!\ll\!n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\!\ll\!n</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mechanism of convergence.</em> The isotropicity of the sketch ensures that the singular-spectrum of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is conditioned in roughly the same as the centralized setting (§<a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Unconstrained (centralized) setting ‣ 5 Evaluation of EAGLE ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>), up to a slowdown of a <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx r/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx r/n</annotation></semantics></math> factor. As in this previous setting, once <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\ell}\leq 2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\ell}\leq 2,</annotation></semantics></math> the same quadratic Newton-Schulz phase takes over, leaving <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/r\log(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/r\log(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> as the main convergence scale with otherwise quadratic convergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
The standard competitor is Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD): GD run on an <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-column sketch. It shares the <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/r</annotation></semantics></math> spectral slowdown but
keeps its native <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> dependence, so we expect a multiplicative
<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa^{2}/\log\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa^{2}/\log\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> advantage in total iterations for
<span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span> on ill-conditioned data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical protocol.</span>
We vary the rank, the single rate-controlling parameter <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S5.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-rank sweep.</span> We vary the rank <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\!\in\!\{n/8,n/4,n/2,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\!\in\!\{n/8,n/4,n/2,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>
on a <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="242{\times}242" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>242</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>242</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">242{\times}242</annotation></semantics></math> matrix with <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=10^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=10^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Per-iteration cost.</span>
The per-iteration wall-clock time decreases with sketch size: 21, 16, 7 and 5 milliseconds for <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=</annotation></semantics></math> 240, 120, 60 and 30, respectively.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Noise robustness (<math id="S5.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>) and step-size sensitivity curves are in
§<a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Further Details on Evaluation of EAGLE ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Take-away.</span>
Iteration count grows linearly with <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/r</annotation></semantics></math>, while time per iter falls up to <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="7{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Against RandSketch+CG the method reaches
<math id="S5.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon{=}10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon{=}10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> in roughly one third the wall-clock for
<math id="S5.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating that sketching <em id="S5.SS3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">plus</em> the
second-order update preserves the speed advantage of the
unconstrained regime even under tight compute budgets.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusions</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We view transformers as fixed data-to-data transforms whose forward pass exposes emergent algorithms. We fix one low-rank matrix-completion task and explore various regimes. Our proposed rule, <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span>  is a transformer-induced method that emerges as a unifying algorithm in centralized, distributed and sketching regimes,
achieving second-order convergence with only
<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is the condition number), <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (distributed data diversity) or <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/r</annotation></semantics></math>
(sketch) slow-downs. Empirically, it outperforms Conjugate Gradient and Gradient Descent by
1–2 orders-of-magnitude in both iteration complexity and net communication (in the distributed setting), and matches the behaviour of QR-based solvers up to <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>log</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log</annotation></semantics></math> terms,
offering a practical drop-in solver when memory or bandwidth is tight.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Limitations.</span>
Numeric stability beyond <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\!&gt;\!10^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">&gt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\!&gt;\!10^{8}</annotation></semantics></math> is untested. Second, we show one
pre-training recipe that yields
<span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span>; extensions to non-linear regimes that do so are open.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/legend_performance.svg" id="S6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/16;" width="333" height="16"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/central_rank_30.svg" id="S6.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/345;" width="476" height="345"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S6.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">rank 30</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S6.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/central_rank_60.svg" id="S6.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/345;" width="476" height="345"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S6.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">rank 60</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
