<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Methodological Details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Nyström Approximation</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Nyström approximation of a block matrix <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\\
B&amp;\hbox{\pagecolor{gray!25}$\displaystyle D$}\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFDFDF;" mathbackground="#DFDFDF">D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\\
B&amp;\hbox{\pagecolor{gray!25}$\displaystyle D$}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> with missing block <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the matrix</p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{X}_{*}=\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\\
B&amp;\hat{D}_{*}\end{bmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{X}_{*}=\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\\
B&amp;\hat{D}_{*}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{*}:=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}C." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{*}:=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}C.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that this approximation ensures that the rank of the approximate <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{X}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{X}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as the rank of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>—in this way, the Nystrom approximation reduces both the rank of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as the Frobenius norm of the error. The latter property can be seen from the fact that the approximation just amounts to a joint execution of ordinary least squares. Indeed, if we treat the <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th row of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as a ‘response variable’ and the <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th column of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C,c^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C,c^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as a query point, then</p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{D}_{*})_{ij}=B^{i}A(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}c^{j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{D}_{*})_{ij}=B^{i}A(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}c^{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">is precisely the estimate when one regreeses the data <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,B^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,B^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. For this reason, we may also interpret the method as computing the estimation parameters</p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{*}:=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}\times d}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{*}:=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}\times d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">and then computing the estimate</p>
<table id="A1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}_{*}=W_{*}C," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}_{*}=W_{*}C,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">much as in ordinary linear regression. This value <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> will appear later in our theoretical analyses.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We note that the approximation above is defined whether the matrix <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is noise-corrupted or not. In the presence of noise, it inherits many of the statistical properties of linear regression when we assume that the noise is restricted to the ‘response variables’ <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B,D</annotation></semantics></math>. As a special case, if the rank of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to that of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math> then in fact it holds that <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}B&amp;D\end{bmatrix}=W_{*}\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}B&amp;D\end{bmatrix}=W_{*}\begin{bmatrix}A&amp;C\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math> i.e., the Nyström approximation is exact. We will sometimes refer to this as the ‘noiseless’ case.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Training specifications</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The transformer is trained using the Adam optimizer with a constant learning rate of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.001</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.001</annotation></semantics></math> and a batch size of 1024 for 20,000 iterations. To stabilize training, gradient clipping with a 2-norm threshold of 0.1 is applied at each step. At every iteration, a new batch is independently sampled as detailed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Method ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, ensuring that the model never sees the same data twice during training or inference. The block masking pattern remains fixed throughout. We observe occasional spikes in the training loss (Figure <a href="#A1.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ A.2 Training specifications ‣ Appendix A Methodological Details ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) and interpret those as occasional failures of the learned solver to converge, typically occurring when <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> attains untypically large values. We recall that, under our data sampling procedure, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is random and unbounded, allowing for such outlier cases.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F11" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F11.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/training.svg" id="A1.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/169;" width="229" height="169"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F11.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A1.F11.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training loss, median across 10 independent runs.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F11.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/qks.svg" id="A1.F11.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/169;" width="229" height="169"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F11.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><span id="A1.F11.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prediction difference when sparsifying weights in centralized setting. For reference, the black dotted line marks the trained transformer’s mean squared error. Mean across 10 training runs reported.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Runtime of attention map</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the dominant computational cost in a transformer layer arises from the attention mechanism:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Attn}(Z)=\bigl(ZW_{Q}(ZW_{K})^{\top}\odot M\bigr)ZW_{V}W_{P}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Attn}(Z)=\bigl(ZW_{Q}(ZW_{K})^{\top}\odot M\bigr)ZW_{V}W_{P}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times(n+n^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times(n+n^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input matrix and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q},W_{K},W_{V},W_{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q},W_{K},W_{V},W_{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> are learned projection matrices. Assuming that the size of the submatrix <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> remains constant, we have <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}=\Theta(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}=\Theta(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}=\Theta(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}=\Theta(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The computation of <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ZW_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZW_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ZW_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZW_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ZW_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZW_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> requires <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(ndk)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(ndk)</annotation></semantics></math> time. The inner product and masking operation <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ZW_{Q})(ZW_{K})^{\top}\odot M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ZW_{Q})(ZW_{K})^{\top}\odot M</annotation></semantics></math> incurs a cost of <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(d^{2}k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(d^{2}k)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the subsequent multiplication with <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ZW_{V}W_{P}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZW_{V}W_{P}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> requires an additional <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(d^{2}k+ndk)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(d^{2}k+ndk)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the total time complexity of the attention computation is <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(d^{2}k+ndk)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(d^{2}k+ndk)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the unconstrained case <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=n</annotation></semantics></math>, this yields a cost of <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(nd^{2}+dn^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(nd^{2}+dn^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, selecting <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math> can significantly reduce computational overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Of course, in our recovered algorithms, we can ‘precompute’ <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q}W_{K}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q}W_{K}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}W_{P}^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}W_{P}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math> and these are further scaled versions of block-identity matrices, and so the computation of <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ZW_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZW_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> etc. can be avoided. This reduces the cost to <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d^{2}k)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d^{2}k),</annotation></semantics></math> which for the unconstrained setting of <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=n</annotation></semantics></math> works out to <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d^{2}n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d^{2}n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the sketched versions of the iterations, these matrices become nontrivial again, and their multiplication must be incorporated into the accounting of the costs of the recovered algorithm. Of course, in this regime, <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=r\ll n," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=r\ll n,</annotation></semantics></math> so the domainting cost is <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(ndr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(ndr)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Details on setup for distributed setting</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design the transformer to emulate a distributed algorithm by constraining the attention mechanism. Specifically, the query, key, and value matrices are restricted to data local to a single machine, and due to symmetry across machines, a single set of transformation is shared across all attention heads. At every layer, the input data is partitioned as:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{k}=\begin{bmatrix}\cdots&amp;X^{\mu}_{k}&amp;X^{\mu+1}_{k}&amp;\cdots\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times M(n+n^{\prime})},\textit{ where }X^{\mu}_{k}=\begin{bmatrix}A^{\mu}_{k}&amp;C_{k}\\[2.0pt]
B^{\mu}_{k}&{D}_{k}\end{bmatrix},\mu\in[1:M]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> where </mtext><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}=\begin{bmatrix}\cdots&amp;X^{\mu}_{k}&amp;X^{\mu+1}_{k}&amp;\cdots\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+d^{\prime})\times M(n+n^{\prime})},\textit{ where }X^{\mu}_{k}=\begin{bmatrix}A^{\mu}_{k}&amp;C_{k}\\[2.0pt]
B^{\mu}_{k}&amp;{D}_{k}\end{bmatrix},\mu\in[1:M]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with blocks <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{n^{\prime}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{n^{\prime}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> distributed across machines, while the shared blocks <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\mathbb{R}^{n^{\prime}\times d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\mathbb{R}^{n^{\prime}\times d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are replicated identically in each <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>. This setup reflects common scenarios where the missing block <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is significantly smaller than the full matrix <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, often remaining constant in size (e.g., <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}=n^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}=n^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math> in regression), making the distributed partitioning both efficient and natural.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The projection matrix after attention is unconstrained, enabling unrestricted communication between heads and implicitly modeling a fully connected communication topology. This architectural design encourages local, machine-specific computation in the attention mechanism, while allowing for global coordination in the projection step. Empirically, we find that the model leverages this flexibility efficiently: although capable of learning full data sharing, it consistently limits itself to communication scaling with <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(|D|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(|D|)</annotation></semantics></math> bits.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We find that transposing the per-machine data <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> is necessary in the distributed setup. That is, we give the transformer inputs <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}=\begin{bmatrix}\cdots&amp;X^{\mu,\top}_{k}&amp;X^{\mu+1,\top}_{k}&amp;\cdots\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}=\begin{bmatrix}\cdots&amp;X^{\mu,\top}_{k}&amp;X^{\mu+1,\top}_{k}&amp;\cdots\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> and train it on the mean-squared error to <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Details on algorithm extraction</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We derive closed-form iterative updates from the trained transformer weights <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. This involves thresholding and clustering the weight matrices in both the unconstrained and distributed settings:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The sparsification threshold <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is set dynamically as <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1.5\cdot\|W\|_{1}/\#\text{elements}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>#</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>elements</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1.5\cdot\|W\|_{1}/\#\text{elements}</annotation></semantics></math>, accounting for varying scales across weight matrices. The constant 1.5 is selected empirically and is not further tuned.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The number of quantization levels is determined by analyzing the mean prediction error induced by weight sparsification (see Figure <a href="#A1.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ A.2 Training specifications ‣ Appendix A Methodological Details ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). To maintain performance comparable to the original transformer, we cluster <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> into 2 values and <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> into 3.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the compute-limited setting, a sketch matrix is extracted and its structural properties analyzed (see Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Parameter choices for the update steps are abstracted through empirically observed scaling laws. Specifically, we identify <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}\alpha_{2}\approx 1/\|A\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}\alpha_{2}\approx 1/\|A\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, as supported by results in the main text. Furthermore, we approximate <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}\approx 1.92\cdot\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1.92</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}\approx 1.92\cdot\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the resulting squared prediction error is <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> (averaged over 10 training runs), remaining below the baseline error of approximately <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Emergent Algorithm</h2>

<figure id="A2.F12" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="A2.F12.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">Layer 1                 Layer 2</p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F12.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/qk_average_0.png" id="A2.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/87;" width="238" height="87" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/qk_average_1.png" id="A2.F12.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/85;" width="238" height="85" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/value_average_0.png" id="A2.F12.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/79;" width="238" height="79" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/value_average_1.png" id="A2.F12.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/79;" width="238" height="79" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="A2.F12.4" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">Layer 3                 Layer 4</p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F12.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/qk_average_2.png" id="A2.F12.g5" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/88;" width="238" height="88" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/qk_average_3.png" id="A2.F12.g6" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/88;" width="238" height="88" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/value_average_2.png" id="A2.F12.g7" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/81;" width="238" height="81" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/value_average_3.png" id="A2.F12.g8" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/81;" width="238" height="81" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F12.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>: </span><span id="A2.F12.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Transformer weights by layer, averaged over 10 training runs. The top panels display the weight products <math id="A2.F12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the bottom panels show <math id="A2.F12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. Prior to averaging, sign ambiguities were resolved by aligning the dominant coefficients: large entries in <math id="A2.F12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> were set to be positive, and those in <math id="A2.F12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> to be negative.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F13" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="A2.F13.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">no noise (<math id="A2.F13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>)          modest noise (<math id="A2.F13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>)</p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F13.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/x1.png" id="A2.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/87;" width="238" height="87" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/qk_noise.png" id="A2.F13.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/87;" width="238" height="87" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F13.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/x2.png" id="A2.F13.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/81;" width="238" height="81" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.19702v1/value_noise.png" id="A2.F13.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/79;" width="238" height="79" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 13</span>: </span><span id="A2.F13.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of learned model weights without noise vs. with modest data noise on a representative training run. The top row shows <math id="A2.F13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> and the bottom row shows <math id="A2.F13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F14" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/legend.svg" id="A2.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/18;" width="238" height="18"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F14.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/performance_noise.svg" id="A2.F14.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/328;" width="476" height="328"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F14.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F14.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Median test performance</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F14.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/cond_noise.svg" id="A2.F14.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/351;" width="476" height="351"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F14.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F14.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Median condition number</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F14.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/parameters_noise.svg" id="A2.F14.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/352;" width="476" height="352"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F14.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F14.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Abstracted parameters</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F14.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 14</span>: </span><span id="A2.F14.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The trained transformer solves matrix completion (modest noise, <math id="A2.F14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>) with a unified algorithm over all three computational settings. The evolution of key quantities throughout the transformer layers illustrate the remarkable similarity between the latent algorithms. Mean across 10 training seeds is reported. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Transformer behaviour across training runs</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that the emergent behavior identified in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is robust across training runs with varying sources of randomness, including initialization and training data. Figure <a href="#A2.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ Appendix B Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> displays transformer weights averaged over 10 independent runs, reproducing the characteristic patterns reported in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Additionally, the sketching sub-matrix in the compute-limited setting is, on average, close to the identity, further supporting out interpretation as a random orthonormal sketch.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Transformer behaviour under data noise</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we demonstrate that the emergent behavior identified in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> remains robust under moderate data noise with variance <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A2.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Appendix B Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> compares the learned weights of a representative transformer trained on noiseless data and on data corrupted with noise. Additionally, Figure <a href="#A2.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ Appendix B Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> reports key diagnostic quantities across layers. In both analyses, the observed behavior closely aligns with that of the noiseless case, indicating stability of the learned algorithm under modest perturbations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>From weights to updates</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We derive the blockwise update rule implied by the abstracted weight parametrization</p>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{1}I&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\quad\text{and}\quad W_{VP,\ell}=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{2}I&amp;0\\
0&amp;\alpha_{3}I\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{1}I&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\quad\text{and}\quad W_{VP,\ell}=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{2}I&amp;0\\
0&amp;\alpha_{3}I\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and show that it recovers the update structure presented in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for the unconstrained setting. Recall that the layer representation takes the block form</p>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\ell}=\begin{bmatrix}A_{\ell}&amp;C_{\ell}\\
B_{\ell}&amp;D_{\ell}\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\ell}=\begin{bmatrix}A_{\ell}&amp;C_{\ell}\\
B_{\ell}&amp;D_{\ell}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and a single-head transformer layer performs the update</p>
<table id="A2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\ell+1}=Z_{\ell}+\bigl(Z_{\ell}W_{QK,\ell}Z_{\ell}^{\top}\odot M_{\ell}\bigr)Z_{\ell}\,W_{VP,\ell}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\ell+1}=Z_{\ell}+\bigl(Z_{\ell}W_{QK,\ell}Z_{\ell}^{\top}\odot M_{\ell}\bigr)Z_{\ell}\,W_{VP,\ell}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Substituting the block structure into the attention term yields</p>
<table id="A2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{\ell}W_{QK,\ell}Z_{\ell}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{\ell}W_{QK,\ell}Z_{\ell}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{1}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}&amp;\cdot\\
\alpha_{1}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}&amp;\cdot\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{1}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}&amp;\cdot\\
\alpha_{1}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}&amp;\cdot\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody><tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left" style="white-space:normal;" colspan="5">where the entries marked <math id="A2.EGx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math> are not needed due to masking. Applying the attention mask,</td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{\ell}W_{QK,\ell}Z_{\ell}^{\top}\odot M_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{\ell}W_{QK,\ell}Z_{\ell}^{\top}\odot M_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{1}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}&amp;0\\
\alpha_{1}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{1}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}&amp;0\\
\alpha_{1}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody><tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left" style="white-space:normal;" colspan="5">Further, we compute</td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle ZW_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle ZW_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{2}A_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{3}C_{\ell}\\
\alpha_{2}B_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{3}D_{\ell}\end{bmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{2}A_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{3}C_{\ell}\\
\alpha_{2}B_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{3}D_{\ell}\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody><tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left" style="white-space:normal;" colspan="5">Multiplying these terms and adding to <math id="A2.EGx1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the update:</td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=Z_{\ell}+\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{1}\alpha_{2}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}A_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}C_{\ell}\\
\alpha_{1}\alpha_{2}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}A_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}C_{\ell}\end{bmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Z_{\ell}+\begin{bmatrix}\alpha_{1}\alpha_{2}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}A_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}C_{\ell}\\
\alpha_{1}\alpha_{2}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}A_{\ell}&amp;\alpha_{1}\alpha_{3}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}C_{\ell}\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">This clean separation of updates across the blocks <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> a posteriori motivates the choice of block decomposition of <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> used in our notation throughout the model layers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Interpreting the emergent algorithm</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work interprets EAGLE as gradient descent with preconditioning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vladymyrov et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>. In particular, these works frame the method as comprising two distinct phases: a preconditioning step involving only the updates to <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (this is achieved by setting <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math>), followed by a single gradient descent step using the data <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{\ell},B_{\ell},C_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{\ell},B_{\ell},C_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. This perspective treats the conditioning phase as auxiliary and external to the actual optimization step.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, this interpretation does not accurately reflect the behavior of the trained transformer. The model does not explicitly separate conditioning and update phases; instead, it interleaves them continuously throughout the computation. Rather than implementing classical preconditioning followed by gradient descent, the learned procedure functions as a continuous conditioning mechanism that shapes the dynamics of the optimization process at every layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, this prior viewpoint underemphasizes the internal structure and complexity of the conditioning dynamics themselves. In fact, the iteration bears a close resemblance to the Newton–Schultz method for matrix inversion, suggesting a fundamentally different mechanism at play.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To make this connection explicit, consider the unconstrained version of the iteration. Assume <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=3</annotation></semantics></math>, as in the statement of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 5.1 Unconstrained (centralized) setting ‣ 5 Evaluation of EAGLE ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Then, re-normalize <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{A}_{\ell}=A_{\ell}/\|A_{\ell}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{A}_{\ell}=A_{\ell}/\|A_{\ell}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> at every iteration. The update to the iterates <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{A}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{A}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> in this setup can then be expressed in the compact form</p>
<table id="A2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{A}_{\ell+1}=\frac{1}{2}(3I-\bar{A}_{\ell}\bar{A}_{\ell}^{\top})\bar{A}_{\ell}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{A}_{\ell+1}=\frac{1}{2}(3I-\bar{A}_{\ell}\bar{A}_{\ell}^{\top})\bar{A}_{\ell},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which is exactly the Newton–Schultz iteration for approximate matrix inversion, as presented in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Higham,, 2008</a>, Section 5.3)</cite>. This reformulation reveals that the driving force behind the iteration is more accurately characterized as implicit matrix inversion rather than traditional gradient descent updates.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Parameter Estimation</h2>

<figure id="A3.F16" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F16.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
<p id="A3.F16.fig1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.F16.fig1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_phantom"><span style="visibility:hidden"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/legend_performance_hybrid.svg" id="A3.F16.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/12;" width="229" height="12"></object></span></span><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/estimation.svg" id="A3.F16.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/166;" width="229" height="166"></object></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F16.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 15</span>: </span><span id="A3.F16.fig1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Convergence of EAGLE adapted to the estimation problem: unconstrained setting, with parameters <math id="A3.F16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=d=240" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=d=240</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.F16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}=d^{\prime}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}=d^{\prime}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.F16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(A)=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(A)=100</annotation></semantics></math>. Mean over 50 runs is reported.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F16.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/legend_performance_hybrid.svg" id="A3.F16.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/12;" width="229" height="12"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19702v1/hybrid.svg" id="A3.F16.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/165;" width="229" height="165"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F16.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 16</span>: </span><span id="A3.F16.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The accelerated EAGLE for distributed computing largely outperforms EAGLE in runtime. In this data generation, the data diversity <math id="A3.F16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\approx 0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\approx 0.1</annotation></semantics></math> is relatively low. Mean over 50 runs is reported, <math id="A3.F16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=d=240" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=d=240</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.F16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}=d^{\prime}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}=d^{\prime}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.F16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(A)=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(A)=100</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F17" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A3.F17.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.19702v1/qk_est.png" id="A3.F17.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/446;" width="476" height="446" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F17.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><math id="A3.F17.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{QK,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{QK,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A3.F17.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.19702v1/qk_b_est.png" id="A3.F17.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/446;" width="476" height="446" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F17.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><math id="A3.F17.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{B,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{B,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A3.F17.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.19702v1/value_est.png" id="A3.F17.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/434;" width="476" height="434" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F17.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><math id="A3.F17.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{WP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{WP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F17.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 17</span>: </span><span id="A3.F17.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Learned transformer weights in the estimation setting. The transformer architecture is modified and a bias term is introduced: <math id="A3.F17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\ell+1}=Z_{\ell}+\bigl((Z_{\ell}W_{QK,\ell}+W_{B,\ell})Z_{\ell}^{\top}\odot M_{\ell}\bigr)Z_{\ell}\,W_{VP,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.330em" minsize="1.330em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.330em" minsize="1.330em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\ell+1}=Z_{\ell}+\bigl((Z_{\ell}W_{QK,\ell}+W_{B,\ell})Z_{\ell}^{\top}\odot M_{\ell}\bigr)Z_{\ell}\,W_{VP,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.F17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a rank-2 bias term whose first <math id="A3.F17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> rows are identical by design.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the standard prediction task in which the hidden block <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is to be recovered, one may also be interested in estimating the matrix <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\star}=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\star}=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the least-squares variant of our Nyström formulation—where <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to a scalar <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> block—this corresponds to solving the classical least-squares problem. While it is straightforward to recover <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> by setting <math id="A3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=I</annotation></semantics></math> in EAGLE (Algorithm <a href="#algofigure1" title="List of algofigures 1 ‣ 4 Emergent Algorithm ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), so that the prediction becomes <math id="A3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}C=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}=W^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}C=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}=W^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, this approach is inefficient in terms of memory and runtime. It is thus natural to ask whether the estimation task can be achieved more efficiently, with reduced overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate this question, we consider an adapted transformer architecture trained on a matrix completion problem posed as</p>
<table id="A3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\begin{bmatrix}A^{\top}&amp;B^{\top}\\
?&amp;0\end{bmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>?</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\begin{bmatrix}A^{\top}&amp;B^{\top}\\
?&amp;0\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the transformer is tasked with filling the missing block “<math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="?" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>?</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">?</annotation></semantics></math>” with <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\star}=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\star}=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>. We find that a minor architectural modification is necessary to achieve competitive performance on this estimation task. Specifically, we introduce two biases to the query transformation. The first bias is applied when updating the first <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, and the second is applied when updating the last <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We train a transformer on the least-squares setting (i.e., with <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math>) and extract the learned weights, shown in Figure <a href="#A3.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ Appendix C Parameter Estimation ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>. This leads to a modified estimation version of EAGLE, defined by the following iterative updates:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex23X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex23X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex23X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=A-\eta\rho_{\ell}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}A_{\ell}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=A-\eta\rho_{\ell}A_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}A_{\ell},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex23Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex23Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex23Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=B-\eta\rho_{\ell}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}A_{\ell}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=B-\eta\rho_{\ell}B_{\ell}A_{\ell}^{\top}A_{\ell},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex23Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex23Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex23Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=W_{\ell}-\gamma\rho_{\ell}(W_{\ell}A_{\ell}-B_{\ell})A_{\ell}^{\top}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=W_{\ell}-\gamma\rho_{\ell}(W_{\ell}A_{\ell}-B_{\ell})A_{\ell}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">These updates can be adapted to all computational settings considered in the main body. Importantly, this iteration is theoretically equivalent to the original EAGLE algorithm in the sense that <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}C=D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}C=D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> evolves according to the update rule above and <math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> evolves according to EAGLE. This equivalence can be formally shown by expanding the recursive updates and applying a telescoping argument, as in the proof in § <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Theoretical Analyses ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ Appendix C Parameter Estimation ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> provides empirical evidence that the proposed estimation iteration (with the same parameter setup <math id="A3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\ell}=1/(3\|A_{\ell}\|_{2}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\ell}=1/(3\|A_{\ell}\|_{2}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=3</annotation></semantics></math>) converges as expected and recovers the same second order convergence as EAGLE.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Further Details on Evaluation of <span id="A4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EAGLE</span></h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Details on the implementation of baselines</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide additional implementation details for the baseline methods used in comparison with EAGLE.
All three implementations compute
an approximation of <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}=BA^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}=BA^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> and return
<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\approx X^{\star}C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\approx X^{\star}C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx1" class="ltx_subparagraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subparagraph">Exact closed-form baseline (<span id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">np.linalg.lstsq</span>)</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This native numpy impementation of the a least-squares solver is base on a highly optimized LAPACK routine. It determines solution to the over/under-determined linear system
<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{X}\|XA-B\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{X}\|XA-B\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The closed-form minimiser is
<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}=BA(AA^{\top})^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>. It returns <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=X^{\star}C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=X^{\star}C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2" class="ltx_subparagraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subparagraph">Gradient Descent.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We minimize the reconstruction loss</p>
<table id="A4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)=\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{m}\|XA-B\|_{F}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)=\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{m}\|XA-B\|_{F}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Parameters.</span> The learning rate is set to <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\ell}=1/(\sigma^{2}_{\max}(A))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\ell}=1/(\sigma^{2}_{\max}(A))</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring monotonic decrease of <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Spectral norms are estimated via two power iterations, which incur negligible memory and runtime overhead. To avoid instabilities caused by near-singular matrices, we add a fixed ridge penalty <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is low-rank, modifying the gradient as <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\leftarrow G+\lambda X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\leftarrow G+\lambda X</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is full rank, we use <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Iteration and Variants.</span> Gradient descent extends naturally to distributed or stochastic data access variants. Each worker computes either exact or column-sketched gradients (<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{A}_{\ell}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{A}_{\ell}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the data on worker <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with subsampled columns)
which are aggregated on a central node, followed by a synchronous gradient step (<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}=1/\max_{i}(\sigma_{\max}^{2}(\tilde{A}_{k}^{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}=1/\max_{i}(\sigma_{\max}^{2}(\tilde{A}_{k}^{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>).
This yields the following iterative updates:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex25X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex25X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex25X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{m}(X_{k}\tilde{A}^{i}_{k}-\tilde{B}^{i}_{k})\tilde{A}^{i,\top}_{k}+\lambda X_{k}\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{m}(X_{k}\tilde{A}^{i}_{k}-\tilde{B}^{i}_{k})\tilde{A}^{i,\top}_{k}+\lambda X_{k}\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex25X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(gradient + ridge)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>(gradient + ridge)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(gradient + ridge)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex25Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex25Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle X_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle X_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex25Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=X_{\ell}-\frac{\eta_{k}}{m}G_{k}\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=X_{\ell}-\frac{\eta_{k}}{m}G_{k}\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex25Xa.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(synchronous update)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>(synchronous update)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(synchronous update)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Training stops when the iterate <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> reaches a predefined tolerance or after a fixed maximum number of iterations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3" class="ltx_subparagraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subparagraph">Conjugate Gradient Method.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement the Conjugate Gradient (CG) method to solve the normal equations arising in Nyström estimation. The objective is to find <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> minimizing the empirical loss <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{m}\|XA-B\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{m}\|XA-B\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the Gram matrix <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=AA^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=AA^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and the right-hand side <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=BA^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=BA^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, and solve the system <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="XG=B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">XG=B</annotation></semantics></math> using CG.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Iterations.</span> The standard CG updates are as follows:</p>
<table id="A4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=B-X_{k}G,\qquad\text{if }k=0:\;P_{0}=R_{0},\;rr_{0}=\langle R_{0},R_{0}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=B-X_{k}G,\qquad\text{if }k=0:\;P_{0}=R_{0},\;rr_{0}=\langle R_{0},R_{0}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=rr_{k}/\langle P_{k},\,P_{k}G\rangle,\qquad X_{k+1}=X_{k}+\alpha_{k}P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=rr_{k}/\langle P_{k},\,P_{k}G\rangle,\qquad X_{k+1}=X_{k}+\alpha_{k}P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=R_{k}-\alpha_{k}P_{k}G,\qquad rr_{k+1}=\langle R_{k+1},R_{k+1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=R_{k}-\alpha_{k}P_{k}G,\qquad rr_{k+1}=\langle R_{k+1},R_{k+1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=rr_{k+1}/rr_{k},\qquad P_{k+1}=R_{k+1}+\beta_{k}P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=rr_{k+1}/rr_{k},\qquad P_{k+1}=R_{k+1}+\beta_{k}P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The algorithm halts when <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|X_{k}C-X_{k-1}C\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|X_{k}C-X_{k-1}C\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> falls below a pre-specified tolerance or after a fixed number of iterations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Limitations.</span> The CG method is not directly suited for distributed or stochastic settings. In distributed environments, computing <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> requires sharing all local data across machines, incurring communication costs that exceed the desired <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.P0.SPx3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d)</annotation></semantics></math> complexity. In stochastic settings, CG requires a consistent system matrix at every iteration to guarantee convergence, which is not available when data is sampled independently at each step. Empirically, CG under stochastic updates exhibits unstable and non-convergent behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Accelerated EAGLE for distributed setting</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the distributed setting, convergence is impeded by low data diversity, characterized by <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that the continuous conditioning performed by EAGLE operates exclusively on per-machine data. As a result, the condition number of the global matrix <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is lower bounded by <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, even if the data on each individual machine is perfectly conditioned.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Once this bottleneck is reached, further updates to <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>—the most computationally intensive component of EAGLE—cease to be effective. This motivates the question of whether runtime can be reduced by terminating updates to <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> once per-machine conditioning has converged.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we implement an accelerated version of EAGLE for the distributed setting. At each iteration, we evaluate the criterion <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\mu}\|I-A_{\ell}^{\mu,\top}A_{\ell}^{\mu}\|_{F}^{2}&lt;10^{-10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\mu}\|I-A_{\ell}^{\mu,\top}A_{\ell}^{\mu}\|_{F}^{2}&lt;10^{-10}</annotation></semantics></math>, and halt updates to <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> once this threshold is satisfied. This condition ensures that per-machine data is sufficiently well-conditioned.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ Appendix C Parameter Estimation ‣ Linear Transformers Implicitly DiscoverUnified Numerical Algorithms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> reports the runtime performance of this accelerated variant. As expected, the modified version achieves faster execution compared to standard EAGLE. We note that the number of iterations required remains unchanged; the acceleration only affects wall-clock efficiency. The theoretical guaranteed of the accelerated EAGLE remain unchanged as we ensure that <math id="A4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(A^{\mu}_{\ell})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(A^{\mu}_{\ell})=1</annotation></semantics></math> (to machine precision) before freezing the updates to <math id="A4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Ablations for EAGLE</h3>

